+    {
+      "claim": "The finding-continuity contract does not define how a later explicitly linked actionable occurrence interacts with an earlier supported resolution of the same finding.",
+      "location": "docs/superpowers/runs/2026-10-16-workflow-reliability/2026-10-16-finding-continuity-design.md:16–24; docs/superpowers/runs/2026-10-16-workflow-reliability/settled-tree.json, super-plan-ufz.8.4",
+      "category": "premortem",
+      "external": false,
+      "kind": "GAP",
+      "evidence": "The design merges appearances through explicit references, records resolution against the finding, and mechanically projects outstanding findings from source and disposition records. It specifies unsupported resolutions and silent retries, but no rule or acceptance example addresses a new actionable occurrence after supported resolution. Failure story: F is verified fixed and resolved; a subsequent invocation reports that the defect has returned and explicitly links its occurrence to F. Both the old resolution and new occurrence are valid retained records. The projection needs to distinguish historical appearances covered by that resolution from newly actionable evidence; otherwise inheriting F's resolved disposition can conceal the recurrence. This requires a disposition-applicability rule or semantic decision, not a new lifecycle engine."
